## [CXC-PRINT-IMAGEWRITER] Apple ImageWriter as a direct print_services peer

- part-of: [CXC-PRINT-SERVICES]
- lifecycle-position: proposed
- the-model: a DIRECT print_services peer. A small module (recommend ESP32 WITH PSRAM — page-raster buffering wants RAM) speaks the ImageWriter's RS-422 serial (~9600 baud, DB-9, DIP-configurable) on one side and MQTT/ComposeBus on the other. The Mac is NOT in the print path — the printer owns itself and is available fleet-wide regardless of the Mac's uptime ([DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR]).
- v1-text: `print/text` plus raw ESC control codes — a trivial byte stream, the cheapest possible first slice.
- v2-graphics: an ImageWriter ComposeUX Canvas backend rasterizing a widget/scene to the ImageWriter's dot-addressable graphics — the SAME widget the CYD/Mac render, printed on dot-matrix.
- rasterization-reference: the classic Mac's Printing Manager is the best REFERENCE for the ImageWriter's ESC/dot-graphics command sequences — crib the protocol, do not route through it at runtime. This is a documentation source, not a runtime dependency on [CXC-CLASSIC-MAC-PEER].
- silicon-note: not the $0.10 floor — rasterizing a page bitmap wants RAM; ESP32(+PSRAM) tier, native MQTT, no bridge needed.
- pre-integration-questions: ANSWERED — serial params (see the-model), direct-peer topology (see the-model), and the two-phase build (text first, then a graphics backend; see v1-text and v2-graphics) are the design. No open pre-wiring decision remains.
- evidence-serial-link: EMPTY
- evidence-text-print: EMPTY
- evidence-graphics-backend: EMPTY
- evidence-paper-status: EMPTY

