## [CXC-PRINT-CSN-A2] Cashino CSN-A2 thermal printer as a direct print_services peer

- part-of: [CXC-PRINT-SERVICES]
- lifecycle-position: proposed
- the-model: a 58mm thermal receipt printer, DIRECT print_services peer front-ended by an ESP32 (TTL UART to the printer, MQTT to the bus). Same raw-hardware realization class as [CXC-PRINT-IMAGEWRITER], simpler wiring.
- interface: TTL UART, typically 9600/19200 baud, 5V logic — ESP32 TX into printer RX may want a level check; printer TX at 3.3V TTL into ESP32 RX is fine. Command language: ESC/POS — text formatting, dot-addressable bitmap graphics, barcodes/QR. No OS driver exists; the module IS the driver.
- realization: raw-hardware plus an ESC/POS ComposeUX Canvas backend. ESC/POS is a de-facto standard across cheap thermal printers, so the ESC/POS backend is REUSABLE across a whole device class — define-once-realize-many at the BACKEND level.
- prototype-first: cheaper, more available, more forgiving, and better documented than the ImageWriter — the right device to PROTOTYPE the raw-hardware Canvas backend on BEFORE the finicky vintage ImageWriter; then port the pattern to the ImageWriter's command set.
- silicon-note: smaller raster than a page, so plain ESP32 works with no PSRAM strictly needed; native MQTT, no bridge.
- power-gotcha: thermal printers spike roughly 1.5-2A while burning a line — the printer needs its OWN supply, NEVER the ESP32's regulator, or brownout resets mid-print will masquerade as protocol bugs. Prove power at the part before debugging the protocol.
- pre-integration-questions: ANSWERED — the UART interface and command language (see interface), the realization backend and its device-class reuse (see realization), and the prototype-first sequencing against the ImageWriter (see prototype-first) are the design. No open pre-wiring decision remains.
- evidence-uart-link: EMPTY
- evidence-escpos-text: EMPTY
- evidence-escpos-graphics: EMPTY
- evidence-power-headroom: EMPTY
- see-also: [CXC-PRINT-SERVICES], [CXC-PRINT-IMAGEWRITER], [CXC-HAL-ACTUATE]

