## [CXC-CLASSIC-MAC-PEER] 1984 Macintosh (512K) as a bus peer

- lifecycle-position: proposed
- the-model: the Fat Mac (68000 @ 7.83MHz, 512KB, 512x342 1-bit, Z8530 SCC serial, NO FPU, NO networking) as a co-equal bus peer — an INPUT/DISPLAY peer, distinct from print_services. It rides the compact UART frame ([0xC1][len][ns_hash_hi][ns_hash_lo][type_scope][value][crc8]) out its serial port to a `uart_mqtt_bridge`; it is UART-slaved exactly like a CH32V003 daughtercard, but with 256x the floor's RAM.
- quickdraw-backend: a ComposeUX Canvas backend over QuickDraw (in ROM) — subscribe `throttle`, draw the widget on the 9-inch CRT; own the blink locally (state-on-bus, behavior-in-leaf). NO FPU means integer/fixed-point primitives only (the FPU-absent rule).
- toolchain: Retro68 (modern GCC cross-compiler for 68K classic Mac); FloppyEmu to load, since the 400K floppy is likely dead; the classic Serial Driver (`.AIn`/`.AOut`) async ring-buffer read.
- thesis-note: on the bus the Mac and the ImageWriter are INDEPENDENT co-equal peers — the bus decouples the 1984 hierarchy where the Mac owned the printer.
- related-to: [CXC-PRINT-IMAGEWRITER] — decoupled, not dependent: the two are independent bus peers and neither requires the other, in deliberate contrast to the 1984 hierarchy where the Mac owned the printer.
- pre-integration-questions: ANSWERED — transport (UART-bridge; see the-model), render backend (QuickDraw, integer-only; see quickdraw-backend), and toolchain (Retro68 plus FloppyEmu; see toolchain) are the design. No open pre-wiring decision remains.
- evidence-boots: EMPTY
- evidence-serial-banner: EMPTY
- evidence-frame-decode: EMPTY
- evidence-quickdraw-widget: EMPTY
- evidence-publish-back: EMPTY

